 ten expert ratings call the seat Lean or Likely Democratic for a flip", "Prediction markets price Bennett at roughly 91% to win", "Bennett's $4.3 million haul and outside spending against Kean outweigh his cash advantage", "Kean's ties to Trump on tariffs and Iran, plus stock-trade and absence controversies, weigh on the incumbent"], "rationale": "The evidence converges on a Democratic edge: raters unanimously call the race Lean-to-Likely D, markets price Bennett near 91%, the lone recent poll shows her up five, and fundamentals favor Democrats by about six in a favorable environment. Kean's incumbency and cash cushion keep this from moving further, so a six-point Democratic margin with wide uncertainty fits.", "watch": "A late shift in the national environment toward Republicans, or fresh polling showing Kean closing after the debates, could pull this back toward a tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Thomas Kean Jr. (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Thomas Kean Jr. faces a well-funded challenge from Democrat Rebecca Bennett in a district that is evenly split on partisan lean. Every major race rater now marks the seat as a Democratic flip opportunity, and betting markets give Bennett roughly a nine-in-ten chance. The only recent poll, from September, showed Bennett ahead by five points.\n\nBennett has raised about $4.3 million, including a large third quarter, and outside groups have spent heavily against Kean, who nonetheless holds a cash-on-hand edge. Headlines have focused on Kean's alignment with Trump on tariffs and Iran, questions about a House absence, and stock-trade scrutiny. The race remains competitive, but the weight of ratings, money, and polling points to a modest Democratic advantage.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8072, "margin": 5.29}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8017, "margin": 5.2}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8142, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8286, "margin